WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.700 --> 00:00:10.685
So far we have met matrices, and we have labelled individual entries with indices. Now we turn to some further basic operations with matrices.

2
00:00:12.585 --> 00:00:16.295
The learning goals of this video are the following.

3
00:00:17.995 --> 00:00:21.016
First: to add and subtract matrices.

4
00:00:22.666 --> 00:00:26.645
Second: to multiply a matrix by a number.

5
00:00:28.295 --> 00:00:35.048
And third: to check the conditions for meaningful matrix operations, and to apply the distributive law.

6
00:00:37.098 --> 00:00:49.602
We stay with the café example. The matrix A describes the quantities that Anna, Ben, Clara and David bought on the first day; the prices are still held in the vector p.

7
00:00:51.302 --> 00:00:57.001
Now a second day comes along. The matrix B holds the quantities of that second day.

8
00:00:57.601 --> 00:01:06.254
Rows, columns and unit are the same as in A: the same customers, the same products, and quantities in both cases.

9
00:01:08.004 --> 00:01:13.162
What interests us is how much each person bought on the two days together.

10
00:01:13.762 --> 00:01:25.143
For Anna and coffee, that is two on the first day and one on the second day, so three altogether. For Ben and juice, three plus zero, which is three as well.

11
00:01:25.743 --> 00:01:31.533
Every other entry comes about in the same way. We call the result matrix C.

12
00:01:33.233 --> 00:01:44.713
In the last video we learned to label individual entries with two indices. That lets us state this rule in general: c_ij comes from a_ij plus b_ij.

13
00:01:45.313 --> 00:01:48.862
So the addition is carried out entry by entry.

14
00:01:51.662 --> 00:01:57.922
For the sum of two matrices to exist at all, both of them must have the same dimension.

15
00:01:58.522 --> 00:02:06.416
A and B are both 4 x 3. The addition is therefore defined, and C has the dimension 4 x 3 as well.

16
00:02:07.016 --> 00:02:15.525
For the matrix-vector product, only the inner dimensions had to match. For the addition, both dimensions have to agree completely.

17
00:02:17.375 --> 00:02:22.627
In a business application, however, the same dimension on its own is not enough.

18
00:02:23.227 --> 00:02:35.239
The meaning of the corresponding entries should match as well: the same customers in the rows, the same products in the columns, and quantities in both matrices.

19
00:02:35.939 --> 00:02:46.988
Formally, we could also add two matrices of the same size whose entries stand for completely different things. But the result might then have no meaningful business interpretation.

20
00:02:49.788 --> 00:02:59.825
Let us now also take into account that some items were returned. The matrix R holds the quantities that were returned from the two days we are looking at.

21
00:03:00.475 --> 00:03:09.136
Ben returned one juice: three minus one gives two. Clara returned one coffee: one minus one gives zero.

22
00:03:09.736 --> 00:03:17.131
The remaining entries stay unchanged, because there were no returns there. We call the result matrix D.

23
00:03:17.831 --> 00:03:30.232
The entries of D are computed as d_ij equals c_ij minus r_ij. Subtraction, too, is carried out entry by entry.

24
00:03:30.932 --> 00:03:42.980
No entry of D is negative. Negative values would be mathematically possible, but here they would mean that more items were returned than were bought. That would be a sign of inconsistent data.

25
00:03:45.430 --> 00:03:57.536
We now come to scalar multiplication. In matrix algebra, a single number is called a scalar. In a scalar multiplication, a number is multiplied by a matrix.

26
00:03:58.236 --> 00:04:11.469
As an example, suppose that in a comparable later period every one of these quantities is exactly twice as high as in the matrix C. So here we carry out a scalar multiplication of the matrix C with the scalar 2.

27
00:04:12.169 --> 00:04:18.746
To do that, every entry of C is multiplied by 2: 3 becomes 6, and 1 becomes 2.

28
00:04:19.346 --> 00:04:21.605
We call the result matrix E.

29
00:04:23.455 --> 00:04:33.211
Scalar multiplication is carried out entry by entry as well. Here we can write: e_ij equals 2 times c_ij.

30
00:04:33.911 --> 00:04:41.984
The factor 2 is only an example here. In a scalar multiplication, any other number could be used as the scalar.

31
00:04:43.934 --> 00:04:52.864
Addition, subtraction and scalar multiplication therefore have something in common: they are all carried out entry by entry.

32
00:04:55.664 --> 00:05:06.664
We now look at a calculation that contains both a matrix sum and a matrix-vector product. We will see that there are two ways to get there, and that both lead to the same result.

33
00:05:07.364 --> 00:05:17.970
On the left-hand side we first add A and B to get the matrix C. Then we value C with the prices: that is, we carry out the matrix-vector product.

34
00:05:18.570 --> 00:05:24.015
To do that, we tip the entries of the price vector over the matrix of quantities.

35
00:05:24.615 --> 00:05:37.863
For the first entry we get: 3 cups of coffee times 3 euros, plus 1 slice of cake times 5 euros, plus 2 glasses of juice times 2 euros. Together that is 18 euros.

36
00:05:39.813 --> 00:05:53.595
The remaining rows are multiplied by the price vector in just the same way. Since the rows of the matrix C stand for the four customers, the four values in the result vector tell us the revenue per customer across both days.

37
00:05:55.795 --> 00:06:03.668
On the right-hand side we go about it differently. We first multiply A and B separately by the price vector p.

38
00:06:04.618 --> 00:06:14.060
For A · p we get the vector 11, 9, 12 and 18. For B · p the vector 7, 11, 10 and 12.

39
00:06:14.860 --> 00:06:24.800
These intermediate results carry a meaning: A · p gives the revenue per customer on the first day, and B · p the revenue per customer on the second day.

40
00:06:25.650 --> 00:06:38.952
The revenues can be added across the two days. In total we get the same vector as in the calculation on the left: again the overall revenue across both days, but separately for Anna, Ben, Clara and David.

41
00:06:41.902 --> 00:06:48.874
What we have just seen also corresponds to a general rule of arithmetic, namely the distributive law.

42
00:06:49.574 --> 00:06:57.481
The distributive law is probably familiar from school for ordinary numbers, and it holds here in matrix algebra as well.

43
00:06:58.181 --> 00:07:08.979
With the names used here, the distributive law reads: (A + B) · p equals A · p plus B · p.

44
00:07:09.729 --> 00:07:18.915
The condition is that the dimensions match. That means: A and B must have the same dimension, and each of them must have as many columns as p has entries.

45
00:07:20.815 --> 00:07:32.626
On the left-hand side, then, the matrix sum is formed first. On the right-hand side, each matrix is multiplied by the vector p, and only then are the results added.

46
00:07:33.426 --> 00:07:41.163
In business terms that means: we can first add up the quantities of both days and then value the result with the prices.

47
00:07:41.813 --> 00:07:47.792
Or we value each day on its own and add the revenues afterwards. Both routes lead to the same result.

48
00:07:51.842 --> 00:07:56.686
Matrices of the same dimension are added and subtracted entry by entry.

49
00:07:58.536 --> 00:08:02.643
Multiplying a matrix by a number is also done entry by entry.

50
00:08:03.293 --> 00:08:09.309
In applications, besides the dimensions, the meaning and the unit of the entries have to be checked as well.

51
00:08:11.209 --> 00:08:20.094
And the distributive law holds in matrix algebra too, provided the dimensions match so that the calculation is defined.
